.TH CTIME 2
.SH NAME
ctime, localtime, gmtime, asctime, tm2sec, tmfmt, tmparse, tmsec, timezone \- convert date and time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.PP
.ta \w'\fLchar* 'u
.B
char*	ctime(long clock)
.PP
.B
Tm*	localtime(long clock)
.PP
.B
Tm*	gmtime(long clock)
.PP
.B
char*	asctime(Tm *tm)
.PP
.B
long	tm2sec(Tm *tm)
.PP
.B
char*	tmfmt(Tm *tm, char *layout)
.PP
.B
int	tmparse(Tm *tm, char *layout, char *s)
.PP
.B
vlong	tmsec(Tm *tm)
.PP
.B
/env/timezone
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ctime
converts a time
.I clock
such as returned by
.IR time (2)
into
.SM ASCII
(sic)
and returns a pointer to a
30-byte string
in the following form.
All the fields have constant width.
.PP
.B
	Wed Aug  5 01:07:47 EST 1973\en\e0
.PP
.I Localtime
and
.I gmtime
return pointers to structures containing
the broken-down time.
.I Localtime
corrects for the time zone and possible daylight savings time;
.I gmtime
converts directly to GMT.
.I Asctime
converts a broken-down time to
.SM ASCII
and returns a pointer
to a 30-byte string.
.IP
.EX
.ta 6n +\w'char 'u +\w'zone[4];    'u
typedef
struct {
	int	sec;	/* seconds (range 0..59) */
	int	min;	/* minutes (0..59) */
	int	hour;	/* hours (0..23) */
	int	mday;	/* day of the month (1..31) */
	int	mon;	/* month of the year (0..11) */
	int	year;	/* year A.D. \- 1900 */
	int	wday;	/* day of week (0..6, Sunday = 0) */
	int	yday;	/* day of year (0..365) */
	char	zone[4];	/* time zone name */
	int	tzoff;	/* time zone delta from GMT */
} Tm;
.EE
.PP
.I Tm2sec
converts a broken-down time to
seconds since the start of the epoch.
It ignores
.BR wday ,
and assumes the local time zone
if
.B zone
is not
.BR GMT .
.PP
When local time is first requested,
the program consults the
.B timezone
environment variable to determine the time zone and
converts accordingly.
(This variable is set at system boot time by
.IR init (8).)
The
.B timezone
variable contains
the normal time zone name and its difference from GMT
in seconds followed by an alternate (daylight) time zone name and
its difference followed by a newline.
The remainder is a list of pairs of times
(seconds past the start of 1970, in the first time zone)
when the alternate time zone applies.
For example:
.IP
.EX
EST -18000 EDT -14400
 9943200 25664400 41392800 57718800 ...
.EE
.PP
Greenwich Mean Time is represented by
.IP
.EX
GMT 0
.EE
.PP
.I Tmfmt
and
.I tmparse
write and read a date by a layout, as Go's time package does.
A layout writes one date,
.IP
.EX
Mon Jan 2 15:04:05 MST 2006
.EE
.PP
that is, 15:04:05 on January 2, 2006, in a zone 7 hours west of Greenwich, as the date should look.
Each of its parts stands for that part of the date, and everything else is itself:
.TF Z07:00:00
.TP
.B 2006 06
the year, with four digits or two
.TP
.B "January Jan 1 01"
the month, by name or abbreviation, or by number, with or without a leading 0
.TP
.B "Monday Mon"
the day of the week
.TP
.B "2 _2 02"
the day of the month, with neither, a space or a 0 to make two digits
.TP
.B "__2 002"
the day of the year, with spaces or zeros to make three digits
.TP
.B "15 3 03"
the hour, of 24 with two digits, or of 12 with one or two
.TP
.B "4 04 5 05"
the minute and the second, with one digit or two
.TP
.B "PM pm"
.L AM
or
.LR PM ,
in either case
.TP
.B MST
the zone's name
.TP
.B "-0700 -07:00 -07 -070000 -07:00:00"
the zone's offset from Greenwich, with or without colons, minutes and seconds
.TP
.B "Z0700 Z07:00 Z07 Z070000 Z07:00:00"
the same, but
.L Z
for Greenwich itself
.TP
.B ".000 .999"
a fraction of a second, with as many digits as the zeros, or as many as needed up to the nines;
a comma may stand for the point
.PD
.PP
They take a part only where it cannot start a longer one, and
.L Jan
and
.L Mon
only where a lower-case letter does not follow.
.I Tmfmt
returns the text of
.I tm
by the layout, in memory from
.IR malloc (2).
A
.B Tm
holds no fraction of a second, so
.L .000
writes zeros, nine at most, and
.L .999
nothing.
.I Tmparse
reads the date in
.I s
by the layout into
.IR tm ,
and returns 0, or \-1 with the error string set when
.I s
does not fit the layout or names a day that does not exist.
A space in the layout matches any run of spaces in
.IR s ,
and names match in either case.
A part the layout lacks is the least it can be: January, the first, the year 0, midnight.
Without a zone in
.IR s ,
the date is Greenwich's.
.L UTC
under
.LR MST ,
or
.L Z
under a
.L Z0700
part, makes the date Greenwich's whatever else
.I s
says; otherwise an offset in numbers wins over a name.
With only a name, the offset is the local zone's if the name is the local zone's, and 0 if not, so a name such as
.L GMT+10
labels the date and does not move it, as in Go.
Under
.LR MST ,
.I tmparse
also reads an offset written as
.LR -0700 ,
the form
.I tmfmt
writes there for a zone with no name, so what
.I tmfmt
writes reads back.
A fraction's point may be a period or a comma, whichever the layout has, and at most nine of its digits count.
.I Tmparse
sets
.BR wday ,
.BR yday ,
.B zone
and
.B tzoff
too.
.I Tmsec
returns the seconds since the start of 1970 GMT of the date in
.IR tm ,
by its fields and
.BR tzoff ,
as
.I tm2sec
does not.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/libc/9sys
.br
.B /sys/src/libc/port/tmfmt.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR date (1),
.IR time (2),
.IR init (8)
.SH BUGS
A
.B Tm
holds a zone's name of at most three letters.
.I Tmparse
keeps no name for a longer one, such as
.L GMT+10
or
.LR CHAST ,
and
.I tmfmt
then writes the offset in numbers.
.PP
As Go's
.I Format
does,
.I tmfmt
writes an offset with seconds less than a minute west of Greenwich as
.LR +00:00:-59 ,
and
.IR tmparse ,
as Go's
.IR Parse ,
refuses it.
.PP
The return values point to static data
whose content is overwritten by each call.
.PP
Daylight Savings Time is ``normal'' in the Southern hemisphere.
.PP
These routines are not equipped to handle non-\c
.SM ASCII
text, and are provincial anyway.
.PP
These interfaces are fixed, but all occurrences of
.B long
in the above should be manipulated as
.BR ulong ;
this extends the range of valid times into the year 2106.
